    installed : bundle 005bca6, update number 8 (RAUC's record of the last write)
Slot B  /dev/mmcblk0p3   state: RUNNING, confirmed          (attempts 3 left, installed bundle 005bca6, update number 5)

=== 4. after the reboot ===========================================================================
# ota-status
Running slot : A   revision 005bca6
Confirmed    : yes, this boot passed its health check
Next boot    : slot A (the running one)
Boot order   : A B
Slot A  /dev/mmcblk0p2   state: RUNNING, confirmed          (attempts 3 left, revision 005bca6, installed bundle 005bca6, update number 8)
Slot B  /dev/mmcblk0p3   state: STANDBY: good, the fallback if the running slot fails
    (attempts 3 left, installed bundle 005bca6, update number 5)

--- reading it --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Result: PASS for FR-026 and SC-005 as one command, and for telling a rolled-back device from a normal one.
1. One invocation gives the booted slot and its revision, the health state of both slots in words, the
   version RAUC recorded for each, and an explicit statement that an update is pending (capture 3, line
   3 and the slot's own state), which plain `rauc status` did not give.
2. "Confirmed" is the marker S99ota-health writes. Capture 3 is the case where it matters most: slot A
   would be `good` in `rauc status` before it has ever booted, and here it is PENDING.
3. The retirement of a slot that hung before the health check (capture 2) is now stated, with the version
   that was tried. This is the reason FR-014 asked for, as far as a slot that never reached the health
   script can give one: it was tried and never confirmed. It is derived from the counter, not recorded by the
   slot, so it cannot say what went wrong inside.
4. The numbers are consistent across the four captures: slot A's update number goes 6, 7 (the hang bundle),
   8 (the new bundle) while slot B's stays 5, and the running slot's revision is the one in /etc/build-info.
5. Only the running slot shows a revision; the other shows the bundle version RAUC recorded. For a slot
   written by an update the two are the same string, apart from labels such as fb19ed4-hang that a test
   bundle carries in its manifest and not in its /etc/build-info.

Not seen on hardware, only on the host with stubs: `RUNNING, NOT CONFIRMED YET` (it lasts about ten seconds
at boot), `UNUSABLE`, `EMPTY` and the last-resort note. The freshly flashed card, where slot A has no RAUC
record and slot B is EMPTY, is the case SC-005 most needs and no capture shows it.
